%N A072718 Numbers m such that 12 applications of 'Reverse and Subtract' lead to m, whereas fewer than 12 applications do not lead to m.
%C A072718 There are 12 twelve-digit terms in the sequence. Further terms are obtained (a) by inserting at the center of these terms either any number of 0's (for 178574614218, 277673713317, 614218178574, 713317277673) or any number of 9's (for the other eight terms) and (b) by concatenating a term any number of times with itself and inserting an equal number of 0's at all junctures. Method (b) may be applied recursively to all terms. - Revised thanks to a comment from Hans Havermann, Jan 27 2004.
%C A072718 The terms are not all obtained from the 12-digit term 118722683079 by inserting digits at the center or by concatenation. Number of 12-cycles by number of digits: 1 with 12 to 22, 2 with 24, then 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 10 with 26, 28, 30, 32, 34, 36. A new cycle, and thus new terms, appears with 36 digits (smallest term 102633997022445976230465768310653789). - _Frédéric Fichepain_, Oct 23 2026
%F A072718 n = f^12(n), n <> f^k(n) for k < 12, where f: x -> |x - reverse(x)|.
